              {
                "text": "I'm noticing that the pattern from one corner quadrant gets mirrored into all four corners, with lines filled along the edges. Looking at the grid of odd positions, I can see the corner cell holds one value while the adjacent edge cells repeat another value, and the diagonal inner cell holds a third \u2014 suggesting a radial symmetry rule.\n\nTesting whether the value at each position equals some function of its minimum distance from the nearest edge, I compare examples: in one case the outer edge value spreads across the entire row/column, while a deeper cell value only appears at mirrored corner points rather than spreading. This tells me the propagation rule isn't simply distance-based \u2014 some values spread along whole lines while others stay fixed at specific mirrored coordinates, so I need to look at the source pattern more carefully to figure out which values behave which way.\n\nSo edge points get repeated fully along their row or column, while reflections give the four-way symmetric copies elsewhere. Looking at the second example, the corner 8 sits at the top-right this time with (0,8), and nearby 1's and a 4 define the border and inner square lines analogous to the first case, just mirrored to that corner.\n\nI'm now checking a third example to test whether this nested-square, corner-plus-adjacent-markers model still holds when the corner is positioned bottom-left instead.\n\nRing2 corner (6,2)=4 with adjacent cells (6,3)=8 and (5,2)=8 giving ring2 edge color 8, matching ex1's pattern where the second ring had no edge fill. So I'm mapping out the full symmetric ring structure: ring0 corners=1/edges=3, ring1 corners=2/edges=0, ring2 corners=4 with edges still to determine as I work inward toward the center.\n\nNow I'm laying out row-by-row values for the 19x19 grid, converting the ring coordinates into actual grid rows and columns based on the a,b indexing scheme.",
